    <!-- Tutoriel -->
    <string name="tutorial_title">How it works</string>
    <string name="tutorial_intro">Ten steps, from creating the room to the host\'s soundboard.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_start">Let\'s go</string>
    <string name="tutorial_1_title">Choose your nickname</string>
    <string name="tutorial_1_body">On the home screen, enter the nickname that will show on the board — you can change it at any time. The game runs on local Wi-Fi: every phone on the same network, no internet, and the most accurate timing.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_2_title">Create or join the room</string>
    <string name="tutorial_2_body">The host taps “Create a room”: a five-letter code appears at the top of the screen. The others tap “Join” and enter that code. On local Wi-Fi the room shows up on its own in the list of rooms found: one tap, nothing to type.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_3_title">Call the go</string>
    <string name="tutorial_3_body">The host taps “Go!”. A 3 · 2 · 1 countdown fires on every phone at once, then each buzzer turns green to the millisecond. On the players\' phones the rest of the screen clears for the round: only the buzzer remains, large, in the middle. The host keeps the full desk — it is their dashboard — and plays along.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_4_title">Buzzing</string>
    <string name="tutorial_4_body">The first to press turns red, everyone else locks out instantly. The exact time of the buzz is sent — hour, minute, second and millisecond — and the host sees the ranking, the gap to the best time and the measurement uncertainty. Once the ranking settles, a single buzzer stays green: the one with the floor.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colours_title">The buzzer colours</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colours_body">The buzzer says it all by its colour: no need to read the board to know where you stand.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_armed">Green · buzzers are open, press</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_speaking">White · the floor is yours, answer</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_right">Bright green · right answer, the point is yours</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_wrong">Red · wrong answer</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_lost">Blue · someone else has the floor, or the round has not started</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_eliminated">Grey · buzzer switched off by the host</string>
    <string name="tutorial_colour_countdown">Amber · the 3 · 2 · 1 countdown, get ready</string>
    <string name="tutorial_5_title">Running the rounds</string>
    <string name="tutorial_5_body">Three verdicts sit under the banner. “Right” adds the point, turns the player\'s buzzer green with the correct-answer sound — the host hears it too — then clears the buzzers. “Wrong” turns their buzzer red and sounds the penalty on both phones, without moving the floor; the button then becomes “Knock out”, so the same question can be put again to the players who have not answered yet. “Next player” moves the floor down the ranking. “Restart” opens a new round, and the circular arrow wipes the board clean: buzzes cleared, every buzzer back on, scores untouched.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_6_title">Knock out, revive, keep score</string>
    <string name="tutorial_6_body">The host taps a player\'s row to open their desk: grey buzzer (knocked out) or brought back in one gesture, points in +1, +3 or −1, removal from the room. A knocked-out player disappears from the other players\' boards — they are not playing any more — but stays on the host\'s, who can call them back at any time. The circular arrow, to the right of the go, lights them all up at once.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_7_title">Hand over hosting</string>
    <string name="tutorial_7_body">From that same desk, “Hand over hosting” gives the host role to another player. Nobody leaves the room: scores, statuses and the current round follow, and the former host becomes an ordinary player.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_8_title">Bend the rules</string>
    <string name="tutorial_8_body">The sliders button, to the right of the go, opens the game rules. Fast mode can be turned off in favour of race mode, where everyone buzzes and you get a full ranking — handy for a tie-break or a speed round. “Hidden board” keeps the suspense: each player is left with their own line alone, you keep the full table. The countdown and the sounds switch off in the same place.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_9_title">Prepare your sound effects</string>
    <string name="tutorial_9_body">The “Sounds” button, above the board, opens the host\'s soundboard: nine pads to fill before the game from the sounds shipped with the app. A tap plays the sound, a long press changes the pad or clears it. Your selection is kept from one game to the next, and the panel folds back onto the board.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_tips_title">To keep the buzz honest</string>
    <string name="tutorial_tip_1">Everyone on the same Wi-Fi in local mode — nobody on mobile data.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_tip_2">Turn on “Keep the screen on” in the settings before you start.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_tip_3">The gap shown accounts for network latency: what is compared is the moment of the press, not the order in which messages arrive.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_tip_4">Below the uncertainty shown (± a few ms locally), two reflexes are tied: the host decides.</string>
</resources>
